---
description: How Grok Bot hands a task to Claude ("send to Claude") via the grok-task GitHub webhook
alwaysApply: false
---
---

When I say "send to Claude", or a task should go to Claude, make this HTTP request:

- **POST** `https://api.github.com/repos/Acaydia/workflows-starter-template/dispatches`
- **Headers:**
  - `Authorization: Bearer <GITHUB_DISPATCH_TOKEN>` (the GitHub token saved in this routine's secrets)
  - `Accept: application/vnd.github+json`
- **JSON body:**

```json
{
  "event_type": "grok-task",
  "client_payload": {
    "source": "Grok Bot",
    "task": "<one clear, self-contained description of what Claude should do>",
    "context": "<links, details, decisions made so far>",
    "priority": "normal"
  }
}
```

A **204** response means it was accepted. Claude starts right away and posts the
result as a GitHub issue labeled `from-grok-bot` in Acaydia/workflows-starter-template.
Tell me the status code, and the issue link once it appears.

Rules:
- Write the task so it makes sense without our chat.
- Never put passwords, API keys, tokens or other secrets in the task or context.

Fallback, if the request fails: open a GitHub issue in the same repo labeled
`for-claude`, titled `[Grok → Claude] <summary>`, with Task / Context / Priority
sections. Claude picks those up when the user runs /grok-inbox.
